=0A=0AMore below: two case studies on who carries an improvement, and a fou=
r-column=0Acheck to run on your list before it's final.=0A=0A01The Shift=0A=
=0A=0AName the Person Before You Fund the Project=0A=0AIn a survey Industry=
Week reported on October 5, 36% of respondents named=0Aautomation and advan=
ced technology as their top investment priority for 2027.=0AWorkforce train=
ing came third, at 18%, and nearly 30% said their organizations=0Aaren=E2=
=80=99t effective at attracting and keeping the next generation of talent.=
=0A=0AA machine arrives on a set date. The people who learn it and keep it =
working are=0Aharder to plan for, and some of them will leave.=0A=0AAt SMA,=
 Inc., an agricultural parts supplier, the team that had used Toyota Kata=
=0Ain the warehouse for almost two years took it to every department, with =
help=0Afrom Arkansas Manufacturing Solutions. A NIST case study reports $1.=
75 million=0Ain increased sales, $5 million in retained sales and $500,000 =
in cost savings,=0Awithout saying over what period.=0A=0AAt Green Bay Packa=
ging=E2=80=99s Arkansas Kraft mill, regular turnover means the mill=0Akeeps=
 needing new internal auditors for its ISO 9001 quality system, so training=
=0Athem is a standing job.=0A=0ASMA had people inside who owned the method.=
 The mill has to keep replacing them.=0AEither way, an approval needs a nam=
ed owner and a plan for who learns the work=0Anext.=0A=0A02From the Floor=
=0A=0A=0ANo One Inside to Carry It=0A=0A"A lot of the improvement and optim=
ization decisions in front of stakeholders, a=0Alot of the time, don't have=
 any internal people to support them or champion=0Athem."=0A=0AMadhur Bedre=
 [https://www.linkedin.com/in/madhur-bedre/]=0A=0APresident, Atlas Predicti=
on Control [https://atlasprx.com/]. The Operations=0ABrief Roundtable No. 2=
, October 2, 2026=0A[https://www.theoperationsbrief.com/roundtable/rt02-wor=
th-the-investment/]=0A=0AMadhur's firm helps chemical and petrochemical pla=
nts keep their process control=0Atuned. On the panel he tied the problem to=
 attrition, with experienced people=0Aleaving plants and, in some cases, th=
e industry. He also said companies have a=0Ablind spot past the ROI, in car=
rying a project out and keeping its gains over=0Athe long run.=0A=0AThat's =
one consultant's view from his own client work. It lines up with what=0AGre=
en Bay Packaging is managing at its mill.=0A=0ARead the full Roundtable No.=
 2 recap=0A[https://www.theoperationsbrief.com/roundtable/rt02-worth-the-in=
vestment/]=0A=0A03The Stack=0A=0A=0AAdd Four Columns to Your 2027 List=0A=
=0AAt the October 2 roundtable I asked the panel what one thing a manufactu=
rer=0Ashould check before approving its next improvement. Two of the three =
answers=0Acame back to the people who'd carry it. Here's the check I'd run =
with your plant=0Aleads this week.=0A=0AOn paper or in the spreadsheet you =
already use, add these four columns to every=0Aproject on the list:=0A=0A=
=C2=A0=0A=0AOwner: one person, by name.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AHours: how many ho=
urs a week that person can actually give it, and what comes=0Aoff their pla=
te to make room.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AFirst result: what the team will see work=
ing in the first three months.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ABackup: who learns it along=
side the owner and keeps it running if the owner=0Aleaves.=0A=0AFill in Bac=
kup first, since it decides what happens to the project when the=0Aowner le=
aves. Then add up each owner's hours across the whole list. A plant=0Amanag=
er named on four projects is a capacity problem that no single row will=0As=
how you.=0A=0AI've made the mistake the Hours column is there to catch. I b=
uilt a content tool=0Afor a client team and recorded a walkthrough, and the=
 three experts they'd=0Aassigned all got logins. When the engagement wound =
down, there was nothing to=0Amigrate, because nobody had used it. By the te=
am's own account, the same people=0Acould find 45 minutes for a Teams call =
they didn't need to be on.=0A=0ASend any project with a blank column back t=
o its sponsor before approval.=0A=0AIf you want a first pass from AI, paste=
 your list into the assistant your=0Acompany already approves, with this re=
quest:=0A=0ABelow is our project list for next year. For each project, give=
 me four things:=0Athe owner (a person, not a department), the hours a week=
 they can give it, the=0Afirst result the team should see in the first thre=
e months, and the backup who=0Akeeps it running if the owner leaves. If any=
 of the four is missing or vague,=0Awrite MISSING and say who on our team s=
hould supply it. Then total each person's=0Ahours across all projects and l=
ist anyone named on more than one. Don't guess=0Anames.=0A=0AIt should retu=
rn one row per project with the gaps marked, then each person's=0Atotal hou=
rs and anyone named on more than one project. Read the first-result=0Acolum=
n yourself, because a vague answer can slip through as a note. The names=0A=
still have to come from your team.=0A=0AWhat Did You Think of Today=E2=80=
